Add a constructor to `NoFields` that starts with `super(...)`, passing what `Shape` needs.
diagnostics/inheritance-construction.em:54:5: this constructor has to start with `super(...)`, because building `Shape` takes arguments
      constructor() {
      ^^^^^^^^^^^
Pass what `Shape` needs on the constructor's first line, as in `super(...)`, before anything else.
diagnostics/inheritance-construction.em:55:14: `label` is a `const` field that `Shape` sets
          self.label = "x"
               ^^^^^
A subclass cannot change it. Pass the value to `super(...)` so `Shape` sets it.
diagnostics/inheritance-construction.em:72:5: this constructor has to start with `super(...)`, because building `Shape` takes arguments
      constructor() {
      ^^^^^^^^^^^
Pass what `Shape` needs on the constructor's first line, as in `super(...)`, before anything else.
diagnostics/inheritance-construction.em:74:9: `super(...)` can only begin a constructor
          super("late")
          ^^^^^
Move it to the first line of the constructor, so the base class's part of the object is built before anything else runs.
diagnostics/inheritance-construction.em:78:9: `super(...)` can only begin a constructor
          super("again")
          ^^^^^
It builds the base class's part of a new object, which only a constructor does. To run the base class's version of a method, write `super.name(...)`.
diagnostics/inheritance-construction.em:98:18: this is String, but field `n` of `Plain` needs Int
          super(n: "one")
                   ^^^^^
Pass a value of the field's declared type, or convert it first.
diagnostics/inheritance-construction.em:105:11: `Generated` takes no arguments, but this call passes 1
  const g = Generated(5)
            ^^^^^^^^^
A class that extends another has no generated constructor to take its fields. Give `Generated` a constructor of its own to take these values.
